 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "The Riemann Hypothesis: every nontrivial zero lies exactly on that middle line.",
   "start": 0.7,
   "end": 6.163
  },
  {
   "text": "Not near it.",
   "start": 6.163,
   "end": 6.975
  },
  {
   "text": "On it.",
   "start": 6.975,
   "end": 7.888
  },
  {
   "text": "In his eight-page paper, Riemann called this very probable,",
   "start": 7.888,
   "end": 11.1
  },
  {
   "text": "and moved on.",
   "start": 11.1,
   "end": 12.2
  },
  {
   "text": "On the line, we can watch zeros happen.",
   "start": 13.125,
   "end": 15.462
  },
  {
   "text": "This is Hardy's Z function: real on the line, and zero exactly where zeta is.",
   "start": 15.462,
   "end": 20.312
  },
  {
   "text": "Fourteen point one three.",
   "start": 20.312,
   "end": 21.987
  },
  {
   "text": "Twenty-one point zero two.",
   "start": 21.987,
   "end": 23.812
  },
  {
   "text": "Twenty-five point zero one.",
   "start": 23.812,
   "end": 25.812
  },
  {
   "text": "Computers have checked more than twelve trillion zeros,",
   "start": 25.812,
   "end": 28.525
  },
  {
   "text": "in order: every one is on the line.",
   "start": 28.525,
   "end": 31.112
  },
  {
   "text": "Hilbert put the problem on his famous list in 1900,",
   "start": 31.112,
   "end": 34.4
  },
  {
   "text": "and in 2000 the Clay Institute offered a million dollars for a proof.",
   "start": 34.4,
   "end": 38.5
  }
 ],
